('127.99.d.9.A',4,'Developing several possible solutions for a sagging beam and comparing them follows:','["A random trial", "A guess", "An accident", "A structured problem-solving process"]'::jsonb,3,'Structured processes compare options.','original'),
('127.99.d.9.B',3,'Critical thinking when evaluating a contractor''s claim means:','["Checking evidence and asking questions", "Believing it", "Dismissing it", "Repeating it"]'::jsonb,0,'Evidence supports sound judgment.','original'),
('127.99.d.9.B',4,'Analyzing why a retaining wall failed requires:','["Ignoring soil", "Looking at causes such as drainage and loads", "Blaming luck", "Painting over cracks"]'::jsonb,1,'Analysis finds causes.','original'),
('127.99.d.9.C',3,'A decision matrix helps choose between roofing materials by:','["Flipping a coin", "Picking the cheapest", "Scoring each option against criteria", "Ignoring cost"]'::jsonb,2,'Criteria make decisions systematic.','original'),
('127.99.d.9.C',4,'When two siding options score equally on cost, a team may break the tie using:','["A coin toss every time", "The oldest option", "A random vote", "The most important remaining criterion such as durability"]'::jsonb,3,'Weighted criteria help.','original'),
('127.99.d.10.A',3,'A construction budget should include a line for:','["Contingency for unexpected costs", "Nothing extra", "Decorations only", "Free materials"]'::jsonb,0,'Contingency covers surprises.','original'),
('127.99.d.10.A',4,'A project has $20,000 in materials, $15,000 in labor, and a 10 percent contingency on the total. The contingency is:','["$1,500", "$3,500", "$2,000", "$35,000"]'::jsonb,1,'10 percent of 35,000 is 3,500.','original'),
('127.99.d.10.B',3,'One strategy to lower project costs is:','["Extending the schedule", "Adding extras", "Value engineering", "Ignoring prices"]'::jsonb,2,'Value engineering finds savings without losing function.','original'),
('127.99.d.10.B',4,'Buying standard-size windows instead of custom ones usually:','["Raises cost", "Doubles lead time", "Adds weight", "Lowers cost"]'::jsonb,3,'Standard sizes are cheaper.','original'),
('127.99.d.11.A',3,'A technical report should be:','["Clear, organized, and accurate", "Vague", "Disorganized", "Full of rumors"]'::jsonb,0,'Technical writing is precise.','original'),
('127.99.d.11.A',4,'A technical report on a deck failure would include:','["Jokes", "Observations, data, and recommendations", "Opinions only", "Unrelated stories"]'::jsonb,1,'Reports present evidence.','original'),
('127.99.d.11.B',3,'When presenting a building proposal, speakers should:','["Read slides word for word", "Face the wall", "Use visuals and speak to the audience", "Avoid questions"]'::jsonb,2,'Good presentations engage.','original'),
('127.99.d.11.B',4,'A presenter who anticipates client questions about cost should:','["Avoid the topic", "Skip data", "Improvise", "Prepare supporting numbers in advance"]'::jsonb,3,'Preparation builds credibility.','original'),
('127.99.d.11.C',3,'A crew calculates the area of a 14-foot by 20-foot slab. The area is:','["280 square feet", "140 square feet", "68 square feet", "34 square feet"]'::jsonb,0,'14 x 20 = 280.','original'),
('127.99.d.11.C',4,'If a 4-inch slab covers 280 square feet, its volume is about how many cubic yards?','["1.15", "3.46", "10.37", "7.0"]'::jsonb,1,'280 x (4/12) = 93.3 cu ft; / 27 = 3.46.','original'),
('127.99.d.11.D',3,'A scientific principle applied when a beam bends under load is:','["Tidal motion", "Photosynthesis", "Stress and strain", "Magnetism only"]'::jsonb,2,'Materials deform under loads.','original'),
('127.99.d.11.D',4,'Thermal expansion matters to builders because materials:','["Stay frozen", "Shrink in sun", "Never change", "Change size with temperature"]'::jsonb,3,'Gaps allow for movement.','original'),
('127.99.d.12.A',3,'A successful crew shares a common:','["Goal and clear roles", "Hat style", "Lunch spot", "Tool brand"]'::jsonb,0,'Teams function with shared purpose.','original'),
('127.99.d.12.A',4,'Team performance improves when members trust each other and:','["Avoid feedback", "Communicate openly", "Keep secrets", "Compete"]'::jsonb,1,'Trust and communication matter.','original'),
('127.99.d.12.B',3,'When a layout conflict occurs, using teamwork means:','["Blaming", "Waiting for someone else", "Discussing ideas and agreeing on a fix together", "Walking off"]'::jsonb,2,'Teams solve problems together.','original'),
('127.99.d.12.B',4,'A crew that brainstorms ways to speed up framing is applying:','["Individual guessing", "Delay", "Avoidance", "Collaborative problem solving"]'::jsonb,3,'Collaboration produces ideas.','original'),
('127.99.d.12.C',3,'Compared with a team member, a team leader is responsible for:','["Guiding the group and making sure goals are met", "Only cleaning", "Only measuring", "Doing nothing"]'::jsonb,0,'Leaders coordinate.','original'),
('127.99.d.12.C',4,'A team member''s responsibilities include:','["Competing with leader", "Doing assigned tasks and supporting others", "Missing meetings", "Ignoring directions"]'::jsonb,1,'Members contribute.','original'),
('127.99.d.12.D',3,'A good leader is able to:','["Avoid all decisions", "Criticize without reason", "Listen, decide, and set an example", "Take all credit"]'::jsonb,2,'Leaders model good behavior.','original'),
('127.99.d.12.D',4,'Leaders earn respect on a job site by being:','["Lazy", "Dishonest", "Unpredictable", "Fair and consistent"]'::jsonb,3,'Fairness builds respect.','original'),
('127.99.d.12.E',3,'Employers expect workers to be punctual, dependable, and:','["Safe and cooperative", "Careless", "Late", "Uncooperative"]'::jsonb,0,'These are professional work habits.','original'),
('127.99.d.12.E',4,'A habit that employers value is calling ahead if you will be:','["Early", "Absent or late", "Happy", "Rested"]'::jsonb,1,'Communication shows reliability.','original'),
('127.99.d.12.F',3,'Inequality in the workplace can include:','["Equal training", "Respectful teamwork", "Unequal treatment in pay or opportunity", "Fair promotion"]'::jsonb,2,'Inequality is unfair difference.','original'),
('127.99.d.12.F',4,'Unwelcome comments that make a coworker feel unsafe or uncomfortable can be:','["Recognition", "Training", "Teamwork", "Harassment"]'::jsonb,3,'Harassment is prohibited.','original'),
('127.99.d.12.G',3,'A work schedule helps a crew to:','["Plan tasks and deadlines", "Avoid planning", "Increase waste", "Hide tasks"]'::jsonb,0,'Schedules organize time.','original'),
('127.99.d.12.G',4,'Maintaining a work log each day helps a worker to:','["Avoid pay", "Track hours and completed tasks", "Forget tasks", "Delay work"]'::jsonb,1,'Logs support accountability.','original'),
('127.99.d.12.H',3,'Work is judged complete when it meets:','["The worker''s mood", "Nothing", "The established criteria and quality standards", "A friend''s opinion"]'::jsonb,2,'Criteria define done.','original'),
('127.99.d.12.H',4,'A punch list is used to:','["Order lunch", "Cancel the job", "Add new features", "List remaining items before final acceptance"]'::jsonb,3,'It tracks final corrections.','original'),
