('127.101.d.2.J',3,'Wood ridge caps are cut and fitted to:','["Hold flashing", "Add insulation", "Cover the ridge and shed water", "Support the rafters"]'::jsonb,2,'They seal the peak.','original'),
('127.101.d.2.J',4,'On a hip roof, hip caps are applied from the:','["Top down", "Middle out", "Any direction", "Bottom up"]'::jsonb,3,'Lapped upward like shingles.','original'),
('127.101.d.2.K',3,'Other common roofing types include:','["Metal, tile, and slate", "Carpet", "Glass wool", "Cardboard"]'::jsonb,0,'Many roof coverings exist.','original'),
('127.101.d.2.K',4,'A standing-seam metal roof is joined by:','["Nails through the face", "Raised interlocking seams", "Glue only", "Tape"]'::jsonb,1,'Standing seams hide fasteners.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.A',3,'Insulation is required in buildings to:','["Create heat", "Reduce strength", "Slow heat flow", "Block signals"]'::jsonb,2,'Insulation controls heat transfer.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.A',4,'Energy codes specify insulation levels using:','["Brands", "Colors", "Weights only", "R-values"]'::jsonb,3,'R-value measures resistance.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.B',3,'Fiberglass batts are a type of:','["Blanket insulation", "Radiant barrier", "Rigid foam", "Spray foam"]'::jsonb,0,'Batts fit between studs.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.B',4,'Rigid foam board insulation is valued for its:','["Sound", "High R-value per inch", "Color", "Low strength"]'::jsonb,1,'Rigid boards insulate well.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.C',3,'A wall 8 feet high and 20 feet long has how many square feet to insulate?','["80", "28", "160", "320"]'::jsonb,2,'8 x 20 = 160.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.C',4,'If a batt covers 40 square feet, how many are needed for 160 square feet?','["8", "2", "6", "4"]'::jsonb,3,'160 / 40 = 4.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.D',3,'When installing fiberglass batts, a worker should wear:','["Gloves, eye protection, and a dust mask", "Sandals", "Nothing", "Sunglasses"]'::jsonb,0,'Fibers irritate skin and lungs.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.D',4,'Batts should be fitted so they:','["Leave gaps", "Fill the cavity without compressing", "Are crushed", "Sag"]'::jsonb,1,'Compression reduces R-value.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.E',3,'Ventilation in an attic reduces:','["Insulation", "Roof weight", "Moisture buildup", "Joist size"]'::jsonb,2,'Airflow carries moisture away.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.E',4,'Soffit vents work with ridge vents to create:','["Noise", "Pressure", "Shade", "Airflow through the attic"]'::jsonb,3,'Intake and exhaust ventilate.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.F',3,'A vapor barrier is placed to:','["Limit moisture moving through walls", "Add weight", "Hide studs", "Improve color"]'::jsonb,0,'It blocks vapor.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.F',4,'In cold climates the vapor barrier is installed on the:','["Exterior sheathing", "Warm side of the insulation", "Outside surface", "Cold side"]'::jsonb,1,'Warm side prevents condensation inside.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.G',3,'Waterproofing a below-grade wall commonly uses:','["Paint", "Wallpaper", "Membranes or coatings", "Carpet"]'::jsonb,2,'Waterproofing blocks water entry.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.G',4,'A drain tile system at the footing helps by:','["Raising the wall", "Storing water", "Holding footings", "Carrying groundwater away from the foundation"]'::jsonb,3,'Drainage protects foundations.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.H',3,'Air infiltration control reduces:','["Unwanted drafts through the building envelope", "Door size", "Floor strength", "Roof color"]'::jsonb,0,'Sealing improves efficiency.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.H',4,'Sealing gaps around windows and plates with caulk or foam helps to:','["Increase drafts", "Lower heating and cooling costs", "Raise costs", "Reduce strength"]'::jsonb,1,'Air sealing saves energy.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.I',3,'A building wrap is installed over sheathing to:','["Hold siding up", "Add color", "Block liquid water while letting vapor out", "Replace sheathing"]'::jsonb,2,'Wraps are weather barriers.','original'),
('127.101.d.3.I',4,'Building wrap seams are lapped so that upper sheets overlap the lower ones to:','["Add strength", "Save wrap", "Hide labels", "Shed water"]'::jsonb,3,'Shingle-style laps shed water.','original'),
('127.101.d.4.A',3,'Flashing at the base of a wall is installed to:','["Direct water away from the wall", "Add color", "Support rafters", "Hold siding"]'::jsonb,0,'Flashing diverts water.','original'),
('127.101.d.4.A',4,'Wall insulation improves a building by:','["Making walls heavier", "Reducing heat transfer through walls", "Eliminating framing", "Lowering ceilings"]'::jsonb,1,'Walls lose heat without it.','original'),
('127.101.d.4.B',3,'A cornice is the:','["Door", "Floor", "Trim at the junction of roof and wall", "Foundation"]'::jsonb,2,'Cornices finish the eaves.','original'),
('127.101.d.4.B',4,'A boxed cornice includes a:','["Footing", "Threshold", "Header", "Soffit and fascia"]'::jsonb,3,'Boxed cornices enclose the overhang.','original'),
('127.101.d.4.C',3,'Lap siding estimating depends on the wall area and the:','["Exposure of each course", "Roof color", "Door swing", "Window brand"]'::jsonb,0,'Exposure sets coverage.','original'),
('127.101.d.4.C',4,'To estimate siding, subtract large openings from the total wall area and then add:','["Nothing", "A waste factor", "A paint can", "A window"]'::jsonb,1,'Waste allowance is standard.','original'),
('127.101.d.4.D',3,'Common wood siding includes:','["Vinyl", "Brick", "Clapboard and shingles", "Stucco"]'::jsonb,2,'These are wood products.','original'),
('127.101.d.4.D',4,'Bevel siding is thicker at the:','["Top edge", "Side only", "Middle", "Bottom edge"]'::jsonb,3,'Bevel profiles taper upward.','original'),
('127.101.d.4.E',3,'Fiber-cement siding is made from cement, sand, and:','["Cellulose fibers", "Glass beads", "Paper only", "Foam"]'::jsonb,0,'Cellulose reinforces cement.','original'),
('127.101.d.4.E',4,'Fiber-cement siding is chosen because it resists:','["Weight", "Fire, rot, and insects", "Paint", "Heat gain"]'::jsonb,1,'It is durable and fire resistant.','original'),
('127.101.d.4.F',3,'Vinyl siding is installed with nail slots so it can:','["Stay tight", "Hold paint", "Expand and contract", "Stay rigid"]'::jsonb,2,'Floating installation prevents buckling.','original'),
('127.101.d.4.F',4,'Aluminum siding is valued because it is:','["Brittle", "Rot prone", "Flammable", "Lightweight and low maintenance"]'::jsonb,3,'Metal siding is easy to maintain.','original'),
('127.101.d.4.G',3,'Stucco is a plaster-like finish made of:','["Cement, sand, and water", "Glass", "Foam only", "Paint"]'::jsonb,0,'Stucco is cement plaster.','original'),
('127.101.d.4.G',4,'Masonry veneer is attached to a wall with:','["Wood glue only", "Metal ties", "Tape", "Loose sand"]'::jsonb,1,'Ties anchor veneer.','original'),
('127.101.d.4.H',3,'Before installing siding, the wall should be:','["Painted blue", "Covered in plastic bags", "Covered with a weather barrier", "Left bare"]'::jsonb,2,'Barrier protects sheathing.','original'),
('127.101.d.4.H',4,'The first course of lap siding should be installed:','["Without a starter strip", "At the top", "Crooked", "Level, starting at the bottom"]'::jsonb,3,'A level start keeps courses true.','original'),
('127.101.d.5.A',3,'A steel framing wall system includes studs, track, and:','["Headers and bridging", "Paint", "Shingles", "Plywood only"]'::jsonb,0,'Steel components make up the system.','original'),
('127.101.d.5.A',4,'The channel that holds the ends of steel studs is called:','["Joist", "Track", "Rafter", "Sill plate"]'::jsonb,1,'Studs seat in the track.','original'),
('127.101.d.5.B',3,'Steel studs are typically fastened with:','["Wood nails", "Glue only", "Self-drilling screws", "Tape"]'::jsonb,2,'Screws drive through steel.','original'),
('127.101.d.5.B',4,'Metal-cutting snips and a screw gun are used to:','["Set tile", "Paint walls", "Mix concrete", "Cut and fasten steel studs"]'::jsonb,3,'These are steel framing tools.','original'),
('127.101.d.5.C',3,'Steel framing is often used in commercial work because it is:','["Non-combustible and consistent", "Warped", "Low quality", "Combustible"]'::jsonb,0,'Steel resists fire.','original'),
('127.101.d.5.C',4,'Non-load-bearing interior partitions in offices are often built from:','["Concrete", "Light-gauge steel studs", "Logs", "Brick"]'::jsonb,1,'They are fast and flexible.','original'),
('127.101.d.5.D',3,'A header over a steel-framed door carries the load across the gap so the load goes around the:','["Foundation", "Roof", "Doorway", "Door knob"]'::jsonb,2,'Headers span openings.','original')
